  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "f1",
      "kind": "missed",
      "elements": [
        "system.term",
        "baseline.dependencies",
        "baseline.diff.dependency",
        "baseline.pack.chain"
      ],
      "text": "`baseline.dependencies` makes a glossary entry depend on the terms its Definition or Deviations mark, and a contract element depend on the terms its Represents marks. The amendment changes what those fields mark: escaped brackets now mark nothing. So which elements `diff` lists as changed through a dependency (`baseline.diff.dependency`), and which elements `pack` takes for Context (`baseline.pack.chain`), change too. Neither rule is among the candidates or listed as changed through, because the parameter says \"marks\" in prose, not as a [term] mark.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in 5d4b9f7"
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "system.term",
        "lint.references.term"
      ],
      "text": "The Definition now says escaped brackets in a field mark nothing. It does not say what a mark is, or what name it holds, when an escape sits inside it. Two cases are open: an escaped `]` within brackets, as in `[spec\\] tree]`, and other escaped punctuation in a name, as in `[spec\\-tree]`. Is the name read from the source with its backslashes, or as CommonMark renders it? No Examples row of `lint.references.term` covers either case.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in aee4c0e"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "system.term",
        "system.ref.commonmark",
        "baseline.dependencies"
      ],
      "text": "The Definition of system.term now relies on CommonMark's backslash escapes (which characters can be escaped), but only in prose: it has no Defined by field and marks nothing, so under baseline.dependencies a new Version of system.ref.commonmark would not list system.term, or the rules marking it, as changed through a dependency.",
      "status": "dismissed",
      "reason": "a link only prose makes is left to the coherence check by design (baseline.dependencies); CommonMark does not define the term"
    },
    {
      "id": "f4",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "system.term",
        "lint.references.term"
      ],
      "text": "The Definition says how a backslash escape reads inside a field mark, but not how a CommonMark entity or numeric character reference reads there, such as `[R&amp;D]` or `&#91;spec tree]`. CommonMark resolves both in a field, so no rule or Examples row decides whether such a mark names \"R&D\" or whether `&#91;` opens a mark.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in 64d705e"
    },
    {
      "id": "f5",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "system.term",
        "lint.references.term"
      ],
      "text": "In a field, the tree does not say how a code span that opens inside a mark is read, such as `[spec `]` tree]`. It is undecided whether the `]` inside the code span closes the mark, and whether the backticks stay in the mark's name. The Definition says brackets within a code span mark nothing, but also that nothing of CommonMark's inline reading other than escapes applies to a mark.",
      "status": "dismissed",
      "reason": "no term name holds a backtick; the case predates this amendment; decide it if a tree ever needs it"
    }
  ]
}
